A||||3||overlord||door help||||1||
Z||000000||overlord||12-27-1999||07:38 PM||||when i closed my door toady it wouldnt shut right..the glass rubs on the car (kinda gets cauaght)<P>there is some play with the door (up and down), so i looked at the hinge and it looks worn a little.<P>can i just drill out the holes and put another bolt in for the door hinge?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Wolfhound||12-27-1999||07:56 PM||||The door hinges have two insert bushings each that the pins fit through. They are inexpensive and easily swapped out. <BR> You can do this with out removing the door if you will do the exchange one hinge at a time. <BR> You will need to support the door in some manner, I used a floor jack that happened to be handy. Drift out the top pin then  lower the door slightly to expose the bushing and drift the bushings out. Replace them and the pin if it,s worn.  The pins were fine on mine all the wear was in the softer bushings.<BR> The bottom is the same except you raise the door slightly to expose the bushings.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||12-27-1999||09:20 PM||||be very carefull with the bottom hinge and it's spring. It can hurt you if it gets loose. And it can get loose pretty easy doing this. That sping is about as heavy as a valve spring inside the motor.||||reg||1
Z||000003||dogtired||12-28-1999||12:55 AM||||Hinge pin and bushing kits are available at AutoZone and ORiellys, I found AutoZone about $!.50 cheeper.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><BR>They are in the "HELP"  red bubble pack. For an S10 pick-up, they are about 1/4 inch longer which makes no difference.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I used a grinder and shortened mine. I think they were about $3.50 ea kit. 2kits per door.<BR>Just thought you would like to know, been there, done that. Have FUN!||||reg||1
